            In the broad sense of the word, a
            %em topological string
            is a 2-dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            points to what historically was the main inspiration and still is the default meaning of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em topological strings
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            2d TQFTs, which are each obtained by a “topological twisting” of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFTs"} 2d SCFTs
          %p
            Accordingly, much of “physical”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            has its analogs in
            = succeed "." do
              %em topological string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            of the physical string – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-branes"} A-branes
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-branes"} B-branes
            – have been studied in great (mathematical) detail, giving rise to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} homological mirror symmetry
            and, eventually, the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            itself.
          %p
            Also the perspective of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            as the dimensional reduction of a conjectured
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            – “M-theory” – has its analog for topological strings, going, accordingly, by the term
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+M-theory"} topological M-theory
